task: windows-packed-key
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/windows-packed-key
worktree: ../okay-wt-wpk1
spec: BACKLOG windows-packed-key

why: the entry says a packed store for small integer keys could take
  what §20's hand-written operator takes, and it says MEASURE BEFORE
  WRITING IT. Two things have moved under it since it was filed: the
  completeness rule keeps 93% of panes out of that map, and
  `flows-pane-tuple` stopped building the pair on the path that never
  used it. So the question now is narrower than the entry's, and it
  deserves the ceiling measured before a `PaneKey[K]` seam is threaded
  through three places in code that has been hardened all week.

what: the CEILING first, honestly.

  1. the two map shapes over the same update sequence at the sizes
     that actually occur — `HashMap[(Long, Int), Acc]` against
     `LongMap[Acc]` with the window index and key packed — in
     nanoseconds and in allocated bytes, which is deterministic.
  2. what share of a whole road that map is, from `MeasurePaneBytes`.

  Then one of two closes, and the measurement picks: if the ceiling is
  worth a seam I write the specialisation and measure it again in
  place; if it is not, the entry closes REFUTED with the number, and
  the packing stays where it already is — in §20's hand-written lane,
  where it is priced on every run.

predict: the ceiling is large per ENTRY and small per RUN. A tuple map
  allocates something like 100 bytes an entry where a LongMap
  allocates none, so the boundary panes of the Wroclaw job are maybe
  ten megabytes — against a run that allocates about a kilobyte per
  event over 1.25 million events. If that is right the entry closes
  refuted at around 1%, and the honest sentence is that the
  completeness rule already took what this was going to take.
